<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Teutoburg Forest</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-teutoburg-forest">Teutoburg</a> Forest is a range of low wooded hills in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, running about 100 km south-east from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osnabruck">Osnabrück</a> area to near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paderborn">Paderborn</a>. It gave its name to the Battle of the Teutoburg Forest in 9 CE, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a> destroyed three Roman legions. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> its ridges and valleys were divided among small Westphalian lordships, including Lippe, Tecklenburg and Ravensberg.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/teutoburg-forest.jpg" alt="Teutoburg Forest" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arminia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blick-%C3%BCber-den-Teutoburger-Wald1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Teutoburg Forest is a long, narrow range of low forested hills of three ridges, in the German states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>. It forms the northern edge of the German Central Uplands and extends from the eastern surroundings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paderborn">Paderborn</a> in the south to the western surroundings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osnabruck">Osnabrück</a> in the north-west. South of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bielefeld">Bielefeld</a> a gap called the Bielefeld Pass divides it into the Northern Teutoburg Forest, two thirds of the range, and the Southern Teutoburg Forest, one third.</p><p>The oldest ridge, in the north-east, is Triassic limestone. Deciduous forest covers most of the ridges, and cereals are grown in parts of the valleys. The highest points are the Velmerstot (468 m) in the south and the Dörenberg (331 m) in the north. The river Ems rises at the western base of the southernmost part, and the range divides the Ems basin from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a> basin. A hiking trail, the Hermannsweg, runs 156 km along it from Rheine to Leopoldstal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Until the 17th century the hill ridge was officially called the Osning. It was first renamed the Teutoburg Forest in 1616, by the historian and geographer Philipp Clüver, in memory of the battle of 9 CE, and the name became common after Ferdinand of Fürstenberg used it in his history and had it printed on maps. The Roman historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> named the battle's location as the saltus Teutoburgiensis, a forest valley. Its exact site is disputed, and recent excavations suggest that at least the final stages took place further north-west, at Kalkriese, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osnabruck">Osnabrück</a>.</p><p>After the Napoleonic wars, in a period of national renaissance, German people came to see <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a>, also called Hermann the Cherusker, as an early figure of resistance to foreign rule and a symbol of national unity. The monumental statue known as the Hermannsdenkmal was raised on the hill of Grotenburg near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/detmold">Detmold</a>, close to where the most popular theory of the time placed the battle. Emperor William I dedicated it in 1875. The old name Osning survived among local people, and the part of the ridge around the Ebberg near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bielefeld">Bielefeld</a> is still called by it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Apart from a short area south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osnabruck">Osnabrück</a>, the whole range lies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>. It is made up of two nature parks, the TERRA.vita Nature Park in the north-west and the Teutoburg Forest / Egge Hills Nature Park between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bielefeld">Bielefeld</a> and the river Diemel. Fighting took place in the forest at the very end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. The composer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-brahms">Johannes Brahms</a> liked to walk there while he stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/detmold">Detmold</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Teutoburg Forest” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Rhine-Danube frontier, fixed after Arminius ambushed three legions under Publius Quinctilius Varus in the <b>Teutoburg Forest</b>, marked the northern limit of Roman expansion, with lasting effects on European history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Lippe was a rural territory of small towns and farms in the hills of the <b>Teutoburg Forest</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-lippe">County of Lippe</a></li><li><blockquote>Their territory grew between the <b>Teutoburg Forest</b> and the Weser hills, and around 1214 Count Hermann IV founded the town of Bielefeld beneath the Sparrenburg castle.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-ravensberg">County of Ravensberg</a></li><li><blockquote>The counts of Tecklenburg appear around 1140, with a castle on the <b>Teutoburg Forest</b> ridge north of Münster.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-tecklenburg">County of Tecklenburg</a></li></ul></section>
